## Why This Is Asked

Explores a fresh angle on concurrency: multi-tenant in-order processing with a degradation path that preserves correctness while boosting throughput under pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Per-client FIFO buffers
- Global bounded queue and backpressure
- Degradation path that preserves per-client order
- Graceful shutdown and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code sketch for routing with per-client ordering
class Router {
  constructor() {
    this.clients = new Map();
    this.global = new BoundedQueue(100);
    this.workers = new WorkerPool(4);
  }

  enqueue(clientId, item) {
    let q = this.clients.get(clientId) ?? new Queue();
    q.enqueue(item);
    this.clients.set(clientId, q);
    if (!this.global.isFull()) {
      this.global.enqueue({ clientId, item: q.peek() });
    }
  }

  dequeueForWorker() {
    // pick from current head of a client, then next in round-robin
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test per-client ordering under bursty arrivals?
- How would you verify that the degradation path does not violate cross-client fairness and supports graceful shutdown?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a concurrent, ordered pipeline with per-tenant semantics, backpressure, and dynamic worker changes; requires data structures (per-tenant queues + central dispatcher) and a test plan.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant in-order processing
- Bounded buffers and backpressure
- Dynamic worker resizing
- Late/out-of-order handling with reordering windows

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal sketch showing per-tenant ordering with a map of queues
class TenantQueue {
  constructor() {
    this.expected = new Map();
    this.buffers = new Map();
  }
  enqueue(tenant, item) {
    const q = this.buffers.get(tenant) || [];
    q.push(item);
    this.buffers.set(tenant, q);
  }
}
```

